# Environment for Podman on macOS (`podman machine`). Copy this to .env (see
# README.md), paired with docker-compose.macos-podman.yml — or let
# `setup-host.sh` write it, which reads the two machine-dependent values at the
# bottom off your machine instead of leaving them at these defaults.
DEVCONTAINER_USERNAME=ttc

# The image's user. These are the image's defaults and there is no reason to
# change them on this row, whatever your Mac account's numbers are: the checkout
# arrives over Apple's virtiofs, which reports every file as owned by whichever
# uid asks and lets any of them write (the real permission check happens on the
# Mac, as your account). So neither `ttc`'s numbers nor the user namespace below
# decide whether the workspace is writable here — unlike every Linux row.
# "updateRemoteUserUID": false in devcontainer.json keeps them as built.
DEVCONTAINER_UID=1000
DEVCONTAINER_GID=1000

# THE TWO VALUES THAT FOLLOW THE MACHINE. `podman machine init` creates a ROOTLESS
# machine, and the defaults below are that machine's. The VM runs both podman
# services either way; `podman machine set --rootful` changes which one the Mac's
# `podman` — and so compose — talks to. Whichever it is, this container has to be
# given THAT service's socket, and its user namespace has to be one that service
# accepts. `setup-host.sh` reads both off the default connection:
#
#   podman system connection list
#
# The row marked Default is the one compose uses; its URI ends in the socket path.
#
# PODMAN_SOCKET_DIR: the directory in the VM that holds podman.sock. The compose
# file bind-mounts it through a named volume — the one kind of mount whose path is
# resolved in the VM instead of on your Mac — and tools/docker-socket-access.sh
# links /var/run/docker.sock at the socket inside it, so the local service stack
# works at the path everything already expects. Rootless: /run/user/<uid>/podman,
# where <uid> is the VM `core` user's, which podman machine sets to your Mac
# account's (501 on a stock Mac). Rootful: /run/podman. Giving the rootless
# service root's directory ends the `up` with `reading contents of volume ...:
# permission denied`, because the rootless service cannot read it.
#
# PODMAN_USERNS: `keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000` on a rootless machine — the same
# setting the Linux Podman row uses; the numbers are the image user's above and
# have to agree with them. `host` on a rootful machine, where podman refuses
# keep-id outright (it is rootless-only) and `host` means "no user namespace",
# which is what a rootful container gets by default anyway.
PODMAN_SOCKET_DIR=/run/user/501/podman
PODMAN_USERNS=keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000
